  "Question": "The ratio of boys to girls in Mr. Brown’s math class is \\(2 : 3.\\) If there are \\(30\\) students in the class, how many more girls than boys are in the class?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\)",
    "C": "\\(5\\)",
    "D": "\\(6\\)",
    "E": "\\(10\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The ratio \\(2:3\\) represents \\(2\\) equal parts of boys and \\(3\\) equal parts of girls. Together these make \\(2+3=5\\) parts, so each part contains \\(30\\div5=6\\) students.\n\nThere are therefore \\(2\\cdot6=12\\) boys and \\(3\\cdot6=18\\) girls. The girls outnumber the boys by \\(18-12=\\boxed{6}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
